<h2>What cookies are</h2><p>Cookies are small pieces of data stored by the browser. They can support authenticated access, remember choices or help a website understand how it is being used.</p>
<h2>Essential authentication</h2><p>The current Velmora application uses an authentication cookie to recognize signed-in users and control access to internal pages. Because authentication is security-sensitive, the application architecture should continue to be hardened as part of the site overhaul.</p>
<h2>Preference storage</h2><p>The public site may store choices such as whether a cookie notice has already been acknowledged. Online banking preferences are primarily stored in the application database rather than being represented as a visual browser-only preference.</p>
<h2>Third-party content</h2><p>External services such as web-font providers may receive standard browser request information when their resources are loaded. A production cookie inventory should document any analytics, embedded services or marketing technologies before they are enabled.</p>
<h2>Your choices</h2><p>You can control cookies through your browser settings. Blocking essential authentication cookies may prevent sign-in or access to authenticated banking pages.</p>
<h2>Future analytics or marketing cookies</h2><p>No future analytics or advertising technology should be added silently. If non-essential tracking is introduced, the consent interface and this policy should be updated before deployment.</p>
<h2>Questions</h2><p>For questions about site privacy or cookie use, contact <a href="/contact/" style="color:#315978;font-weight:700">Velmora Support</a>.</p>
